<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danevirke</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Danewerk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Danevirke is a system of earthwork defences across the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, now in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Begun in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and strengthened under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudfred">Godfred</a> around 808 and by later kings, it guarded the southern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and linked to the trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>. It was refortified in 1864 but abandoned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a>, and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danevirke.jpg" alt="Danevirke" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joachim Müllerchen (user Jom) · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danewerk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Danevirke consists of several walls, trenches and the Schlei Barrier. The walls stretch for 30 kilometres, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> on the Baltic coast in the east to the marshlands in the west of the peninsula, and stand between 3.6 and 6 metres high. Excavations of 1969 to 1975 used tree-ring dating to show that the main structure was built in three phases between 737 and 968, at about the same time as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offas-dyke">Offa's Dyke</a> on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Later carbon dating showed that some parts are older still.</p><p>Written sources say that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudfred">Godfred</a> began work in 808, fearing an invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, who had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and Old Saxony. Legend credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thyra">Queen Thyra</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorm-the-old">Gorm the Old</a>, with ordering the wall built, and most of the work in the tenth century is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early ramparts were of soil, followed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> palisade built in 737, later wrapped in a stone wall. The Danevirke was strengthened in the early twelfth century, when the moats were deepened and the ramparts raised. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark">Valdemar the Great</a> then built the brick wall known as Valdemarsmuren, a wall of stone in mortar about 7 metres high on a granite base. It was the last true reinforcement. Danish kings used the structure as a gathering point for expeditions, including raids on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of the southern Baltic. It lost its purpose from the fourteenth century, because of the cost of manning it and the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a>.</p><p>In the nineteenth century the Danevirke became a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in its struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>. It was strengthened with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> emplacements and entrenchments in 1850 and 1861. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> of 1864 no battle took place at it: General de Meza withdrew his army to Dybbøl after the frozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlei">Schlei</a> and marshes made a flanking attack possible, a decision that shocked Danish opinion and cost him his command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The site has stayed in German possession since 1864. After the Allied invasion of Normandy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> considered turning the wall into an anti-tank trench, and the Danish archaeologist Søren Telling persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> to forbid it. The Danevirke and the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Danevirke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Danish kings appear in Frankish sources from the late eighth century: Godfred defied Charlemagne and strengthened the <b>Danevirke</b>, the earthwork across the base of the Jutland peninsula, around 808.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings learned to profit from trade and to protect it, as the Danish king Godfred did when he strengthened the <b>Danevirke</b>, the earthwork across the base of Jutland, around 808.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Southern Jutland was the frontier of the Kingdom of Denmark, guarded by the <b>Danevirke</b> earthworks and home to the Viking Age trading town of Hedeby.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-schleswig">Duchy of Schleswig</a></li></ul></section>
